# The crash reporter: does a failed phase actually leave the evidence behind?
#
# A narration phase that cannot finish is deliberately terminal — no retry, no way to carry on — so
# the one thing it owes the player is a file worth sending. `log.txt` is opened FileMode.Create, so
# the run's log survives only until the next launch: a tester who hits the bug, quits and starts the
# game again to see whether it recurs has destroyed the only record of it. The copy taken at the
# moment of failure is what closes that window, and this asserts the copy is really written.
#
# Driven by the `crash-report` command rather than by a real failure, because a real one cannot be
# staged: the fault this was built for was a socket error that struck twice in 228 requests, and
# --playground answers every LLM call without ever making it, so no script can provoke one. Forcing
# the report is the only way any of this code is covered at all.
#
# The command asserts on its own behalf and fails naming any missing section. It has to: `expect`
# scans the rendered terminal, and a crash report is written to a file — so a script cannot see it.
# What it checks is the PRESERVED COPY read back off disk, not the live log, because the copy is the
# artefact a tester actually sends.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --silent --no-save

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60

crash-report a forced test report

quit
